  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">COVAW</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Chambua project 3rd phase?
Our overall objective is to strengthen the key positive shifts that have taken place; There has beenincreased confidence of rightsholders to raise and amplify their voices on public participation forums through presentingtheir collective demands to duty bearers and that we are also proud of an increase in the number of rightsholders who participate in budgetingprocesses, especially the number of women from vulnerable groups in the fish sector and Persons with disabilities (those with Hearing, Visual ,physical and intellectual disabilities). This  is a critical space for sustained open governance and civic education. Our goal is therefore to consistently amplify the PWDs voices in the budget making processes underpinned by holding the duty bearers to account of milestones achieved as a result to their participation.

A major success is increased meaningful participation of  PWDsand women; and which was a major problem that the project set out to address. We are building on this success in this phase.
Another of our main achievement, and was a challenge, is the already established  good rapport  with the Directorates of Public Participation, both at the National and Counties in Kisumu and Nairobi. This good standing iswhat we are continuing to leverage upon in this phase, to elevate further engagements and discourse around the core issue of public participation, with targeted direct linkage to right holders. In this way, then we also speak to the structural challengesthat PWDs and women have had in engaging in budgeting processes by having more responsive duty bearers. We would like to harvest stories of change from duty bearers on their perspective of PWDs and women&#x2019;s participation.

Public participation and county budget cycles are a going-concern; they are cyclic in nature and focused on specific pillars of development with each call for public participation. We anticipate to sustain this momentum within our current project sites and beneficiaries during the 10-month period; informed by our M and E data of project beneficiaries in filteringover 5 contacts for each project beneficiary. We hope to be able to expand and scale up lessons and sucesses to new project sites in future longer-term programming; while currently, we have continued to share our experiences widely beyond the project sites duringstakeholder LL forums.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="90">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">COALITION ON VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Coalition On Violence Against Women (COVAW) is a National non-profit women#s rights organisation in Kenya founded in 1995 as a response to the silence of the Kenyan society in addressing Violence Against Women and Girls. COVAW has a strong grassrootspresence in Kenya through its community activists. COVAW#s head office is in Nairobi, with satellite offices in Narok and Kwale Counties. COVAW also has projects covering Kisumu, Migori and Kiambu counties.

COVAW is a women-led organisation that prioritises developing thecapacity of women leaders in the workplace and in the communities as a contribution to gender equality goals. COVAW#s main goal is to contribute to a society where women and girls enjoy equal rights, freedoms and thrive in safe spaces. It#s main programmes and activities are related to Facilitating access to justice for survivors of sexual and gender-based violence (SGBV), promoting sexual and reproductive health and rights (SRHR) including the elimination of female genital mutilation(FGM), promote women#s economic empowerment for vulnerable girls as a way to mitigate child trafficking and commercial sex exploitation, and creating safe spaces for girls to develop leadership skills, and promote feminist law and policy reforms.

All COVAW#s work is set to support the most vulnerableand marginalised groups of women, including women and girls with intellectual disabilities, and survivors or violence, particularlyin hard-to-reach areas.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
